    <item>
      <title>The Tragic Conquest of Sloth in the Rigveda</title>
      <description>Today we go back to a time when the four Vedas were still being composed, and one of the greatest sages, Vasistha, lived in harmony with the gods and mortals alike. He possessed an unshakeable calm, a sense of detachment from the world&apos;s chaos. His students would often seek his wisdom, and he would share his insights with them. But as time passed, Vasistha&apos;s fame grew, and his students began to rely on him too heavily. They would come to him for every decision, every problem, and he would solve it effortlessly. The people began to look up to him as a solution to all their troubles, and Vasistha, pleased with their admiration, grew complacent. He stopped seeking knowledge, stopped questioning the world, and stopped living his life. He became a slave to his own fame, and his students, who relied on him too much, lost their own path. This is Sloth — the laziness that comes from being too comfortable, too confident, and too detached from reality. Today we prioritize our work, our relationships, and our growth, ask yourself, are you too comfortable?</description>
